chromium-embedded-framework for Debian
======================================

This package has a NON-STANDARD build workflow that requires manual preparation
and uses BUNDLED DEPENDENCIES for technical reasons documented below.

CRITICAL: Chromium Source Requirement
--------------------------------------

CEF (Chromium Embedded Framework) requires Chromium browser source code as a
build input. Unlike typical Debian packages, this source is NOT included in the
package's orig.tar.gz and is NOT automatically downloaded during build.

MANDATORY BUILD PREPARATION
----------------------------

Before building this package, you MUST manually download the Debian chromium
source tarball and place it in the parent directory:

    # From the parent directory of chromium-embedded-framework/
    $ apt-get source --download-only chromium

This creates:
    chromium_143.0.7499.40.orig.tar.xz  (~969 MB)

The build system (debian/rules) will reference this file at:
    ../chromium_143.0.7499.40.orig.tar.xz

Build Workflow
--------------

1. Prepare build environment:

    $ cd /path/to/workspace/
    $ apt-get source --download-only chromium
    $ ls -lh chromium_143.0.7499.40.orig.tar.xz
    # Verify file exists and is ~969 MB

2. Extract/clone CEF package:

    # If using git:
    $ git clone <salsa-repo-url> chromium-embedded-framework

    # If using source package:
    $ dpkg-source -x chromium-embedded-framework_*.dsc

3. Build package:

    $ cd chromium-embedded-framework/
    $ dpkg-buildpackage -us -uc

The build process will:
- Step 1: Clone CEF repository (branch 7499) using automate-git.py
- Step 2: Extract chromium tarball to chromium_src/chromium/
- Step 3: Apply Debian patches to CEF (using quilt)
- Step 4: Build CEF binary distribution

Expected build time: 2-3 hours on modern hardware
Required disk space: ~40 GB total (Chromium ~6GB extracted + CEF build ~34GB)

================================================================================
BUNDLED DEPENDENCIES JUSTIFICATION
================================================================================

This package bundles several components that deviate from standard Debian
practice. Each bundled dependency is documented below with technical
justification.

1. LIBC++ (C++ Standard Library)
---------------------------------

Status: SYSTEM (libc++-19-dev) - Build 197+
Reason: Aligned with Debian Chromium (use_custom_libcxx=false)

CRITICAL DISCOVERY (2025-11-18):

Previous documentation incorrectly stated Debian Chromium uses bundled libc++.
Reality: Debian Chromium uses SYSTEM libc++ with use_custom_libcxx=false.

Builds 108-113 failed with system libc++ due to missing compiler flags, NOT fundamental incompatibility.
Build 197+ successfully uses system libc++ with proper configuration.

TECHNICAL DETAILS (Historical - Builds 108-196):

Chromium/CEF uses a custom libc++ build with Chromium-specific ABI settings:
  - ABI Namespace: std::__Cr:: (Chromium-specific)
  - ABI Version: 2
  - Custom configuration flags (ASAN instrumentation, hardening mode, etc.)

Debian's system libc++ (libc++-dev package) uses:
  - ABI Namespace: std::__1:: (standard LLVM)
  - ABI Version: 1
  - Different feature set (wide characters, localization)

ATTEMPTED SOLUTIONS (All Failed):

During package development, we extensively tested system libc++ alternatives:

  Build 108: System libc++ only
    Result: FAILED at task 78/77621
    Error: "implicit instantiation of undefined template 'std::basic_ostream'"
    Cause: ABI namespace mismatch (looking for std::__Cr::, found std::__1::)

  Build 109: Chromium's perfetto __config_site
    Result: FAILED at task 56/77627 (REGRESSION from Build 108)
    Error: "_LIBCPP_INSTRUMENTED_WITH_ASAN" empty macro breaks preprocessor
    Cause: Perfetto config has problematic empty macro definitions

  Build 110: HYBRID - system + namespace override (__1 → __Cr)
    Result: Reached task 102/77590 (BEST), but FAILED
    Error: "undefined reference to std::__Cr::setw/setfill"
    Cause: ABI version mismatch (symbols mangled with v2, config has v1)

  Build 111: HYBRID - system + namespace + version overrides
    Result: FAILED at task 71/77643 (REGRESSION from Build 110)
    Cause: Additional overrides created more symbol conflicts

  Build 113: Attempted Build 110 replica
    Result: FAILED at task 76/77683 with DIFFERENT errors
    Error: "no type named 'wstring' in namespace 'std'"
    Cause: System libc++ headers provide only forward declarations,
           not complete implementations required by Chromium

CONCLUSION:

System libc++ with manual overrides requires 4+ sed commands and still fails
due to incomplete header implementations. The wstring paradox (Build 113 error
despite _LIBCPP_HAS_NO_WIDE_CHARACTERS being undefined) proves system headers
are fundamentally incompatible with Chromium's expectations.

PRECEDENT:

Debian's src:chromium package uses system libc++ (use_custom_libcxx=false). CEF now follows the same approach (Build 197+).

IMPLEMENTATION:

debian/rules copies libc++ configuration from Chromium's perfetto submodule:
  chromium/src/third_party/perfetto/buildtools/libcxx_config/

Files bundled:
  - __config_site (ABI configuration)
  - __assertion_handler (custom assertion behavior)

These are placed at:
  chromium/src/buildtools/third_party/libc++/

Header files remain in Chromium tarball at:
  chromium/src/third_party/libc++/src/include/

CRITICAL FIX - Feature Macros (Step 4):

Perfetto's __config_site only UNDEFINES negative feature guards but doesn't
DEFINE positive equivalents. This causes conditional compilation guards to fail:

  Example: /* #undef _LIBCPP_HAS_NO_THREADS */

Without explicit definition, code checking "#if _LIBCPP_HAS_THREADS" evaluates
to false (undefined = 0), disabling threading features.

debian/rules Step 4 (lines 247-260) adds positive definitions:
  - _LIBCPP_HAS_LOCALIZATION 1
  - _LIBCPP_HAS_MONOTONIC_CLOCK 1
  - _LIBCPP_HAS_WIDE_CHARACTERS 1
  - _LIBCPP_HAS_FILESYSTEM 1
  - _LIBCPP_HAS_THREADS 1

This fix resolved compilation errors across ALL C++ components (abseil-cpp,
ICU, protobuf, etc.) discovered during Builds 117-123.

LINTIAN OVERRIDE:

This will trigger: embedded-library usr/lib/libcef.so: libc++

Override justified because:
  - Chromium-specific ABI incompatible with system libc++
  - Upstream requirement, not packaging choice
  - Same approach as Debian's chromium package
  - Extensively tested alternatives (Builds 108-113)

2. DEPOT_TOOLS (Build Automation Tools)
----------------------------------------

Status: BUNDLED at chromium_embedded_framework/depot_tools/
Reason: NETWORK ISOLATION and BUILD REPRODUCIBILITY

TECHNICAL DETAILS:

depot_tools is Google's build automation toolkit (git, gclient, ninja, etc.).
CEF's automate-git.py expects depot_tools in PATH.

ATTEMPTED SOLUTIONS:

  Approach 1: Download depot_tools during build
    Result: VIOLATES Debian Policy
    Reason: Builds must be reproducible without network access

  Approach 2: System packages (git, python3, etc.)
    Result: FAILED - missing gclient, custom git wrappers
    Reason: depot_tools is not a standard system package

  Approach 3: Bundle depot_tools
    Result: SUCCESS
    Benefits:
      - Offline builds (network isolated)
      - Version pinned (reproducible)
      - Matches upstream CEF requirements

IMPLEMENTATION:

Build-time bundling:
  1. automate-git.py clones depot_tools if not present
  2. debian/rules PRESERVES depot_tools/ across clean
  3. Only chromium_src/ is removed during clean

This is Phase 1 approach. Future plans:

  Phase 2 - Include in orig.tar.xz:
    Bundle depot_tools in CEF source package to eliminate manual setup.
    Pros: Simpler for builders, fully reproducible.
    Cons: Larger source package (~50MB), version may drift from upstream.

  Phase 3 - Separate Debian package (ideal):
    Create dedicated depot-tools Debian package.
    Blocked by: Google's rolling release model (no stable versions).

DEBIAN POLICY COMPLIANCE:

depot_tools is treated as a BUILD TOOL (like autoconf), not a library.
It is NOT installed to the system, only used during package build.

Files in binary package come from CEF/Chromium compilation, NOT depot_tools.

3. ROLLUP (JavaScript Bundler)
-------------------------------

Status: USES SYSTEM PACKAGE (rollup)
Reason: EXAMPLE OF SYSTEM LIBRARY USAGE

This is included as a POSITIVE example - we DO use system libraries when viable.

Chromium expects @rollup/wasm-node (contains pre-compiled WASM binary, stripped
from Debian tarball per policy). We successfully use system rollup via symlink:

  debian/rules creates:
    chromium/src/third_party/devtools-frontend/src/node_modules/@rollup/wasm-node/dist/bin/rollup
    → symlink to /usr/share/nodejs/rollup/dist/bin/rollup

  debian/control:
    Build-Depends: rollup

Result: BUILD.gn files find system rollup at expected path, no patches needed.

================================================================================
WHY THIS NON-STANDARD APPROACH?
================================================================================

Debian Bug #893448 (filed 2018, still open) requests a chromium-source binary
package to solve the chromium tarball problem. Until that exists, manual setup
is required.

Alternative approaches were considered and rejected:

❌ Embed chromium in CEF source package:
   - Would create ~1GB source package (unacceptable for Debian)
   - Violates Debian Policy: "do not put code where canonical version is elsewhere"
   - Duplicates Debian's chromium security patches

❌ Download chromium during build:
   - Violates Debian Policy: builds must be reproducible without network
   - Source packages must be buildable offline

✅ Current approach (manual chromium tarball):
   - Uses Debian's official chromium source (includes security patches)
   - Complies with Debian Policy (no embedded code, no network builds)
   - Documented workflow for maintainers/builders
   - Migration path when Bug #893448 is resolved

================================================================================
MODIFYING AND PATCHING
================================================================================

CEF-Specific Patches
--------------------

Patches applied to CEF source (not Chromium):

  Location: debian/patches/
  Format: quilt patch series
  Applied: During Step 3 of build (after CEF checkout, before compilation)

To modify CEF patches:

  $ cd chromium-embedded-framework/
  $ dpkg-buildpackage --build=source  # Extract source only
  $ cd chromium_src/cef/
  $ export QUILT_PATCHES=../../debian/patches
  $ quilt push -a    # Apply all patches
  $ quilt new my-fix.patch
  $ quilt add path/to/file
  $ vi path/to/file  # Make changes
  $ quilt refresh
  $ quilt header -e  # Add patch description
  $ cd ../..
  $ dpkg-source --commit

Chromium Build Configuration
-----------------------------

Chromium-specific build settings (not patches to code):

  Location: debian/rules lines 190-270
  Purpose: Configure libc++, rollup, BUILD.gn stubs

Key configuration sections:

  Lines 197-206: libc++ BUILD.gn stub creation
  Lines 229-244: libc++ __config_site setup (BUNDLED - see justification above)
  Lines 250-261: Rollup symlink (SYSTEM - see example above)

To modify libc++ configuration:

  WARNING: See "BUNDLED DEPENDENCIES JUSTIFICATION" section above.
  System libc++ has been extensively tested and FAILS.
  Only modify if you have new information about libc++ compatibility.

  If modifying anyway:
    1. Edit debian/rules lines 229-244
    2. Document reasoning in debian/changelog
    3. Reference this README.source section
    4. Test build thoroughly (expect failures)

To add new system library substitutions:

  PREFERRED: Follow rollup example (lines 250-261)
    - Create symlink at path BUILD.gn expects
    - Add Build-Depends in debian/control
    - Document in this README.source

  AVOID: Patching BUILD.gn files
    - Requires maintaining patches across upstream updates
    - Fragile to Chromium version changes

Version Compatibility
---------------------

CEF branch 7499 requires Chromium 143.0.7499.x sources.

The build system includes version compatibility fixes:
- CEF expects 143.0.7499.110 (upstream Chromium tag)
- Debian has 143.0.7499.40 (with Debian patches)
- debian/rules patches CHROMIUM_BUILD_COMPATIBILITY.txt during build

When updating to new CEF/Chromium versions:
1. Update CEF_BRANCH in debian/rules
2. Update chromium version in debian/rules --chromium-archive path
3. Update SHA256 hash in debian/rules --chromium-archive-sha256
4. Re-test bundled dependency assumptions (libc++ ABI may change)

================================================================================
ADVANCED BUILD TOPICS
================================================================================

GBP (git-buildpackage) Workflow
--------------------------------

This package is PARTIALLY compatible with gbp:

    $ gbp buildpackage --git-prebuild="test -f ../chromium_*.orig.tar.xz"

The .gbp.conf file includes a prebuild check to verify chromium tarball exists.

However, standard "gbp import-orig" workflow is NOT supported because:
- CEF orig.tar.gz contains only CEF source (~50MB)
- Chromium tarball is a build input, NOT part of the source package
- No mechanism exists to auto-download build-time source dependencies

pbuilder/sbuild Compatibility
------------------------------

NOT YET SUPPORTED.

These tools require special setup to make the chromium tarball available inside
the build chroot. Possible approaches:

1. Copy tarball into chroot before build:
    $ sudo cp chromium_*.orig.tar.xz /var/cache/pbuilder/build/*/tmp/buildd/*/..

2. Use bind mount to make tarball accessible

3. Wait for Debian Bug #893448 to be resolved (chromium-source package)

Debugging Build Issues
----------------------

Build log locations:
- automate-git.py output: shown in dpkg-buildpackage output
- CEF patch application: shown during Step 3 of build
- Chromium compilation: shown during Step 4 of build

Common issues:

1. "chromium tarball not found":
   → Run apt-get source --download-only chromium in parent directory

2. "Patch application failed":
   → Check debian/patches/series is correct
   → Verify patches apply: cd chromium_src/cef && quilt push -a

3. "Version mismatch":
   → Update CHROMIUM_BUILD_COMPATIBILITY.txt patch in debian/rules

4. "Disk space full":
   → Build requires ~40GB free space

5. "libc++ compilation errors" (std::__1:: vs std::__Cr::):
   → DO NOT attempt to use system libc++ (see BUNDLED DEPENDENCIES section)
   → Verify perfetto __config_site is being used (check build log)

6. "depot_tools lockfile errors":
   → Development issue only (not in clean builds)
   → Run: rm -f depot_tools/external_bin/gsutil/*.locked

================================================================================
FUTURE MIGRATION
================================================================================

When Debian Bug #893448 is resolved and chromium-source package exists:

debian/control will change to:
    Build-Depends: chromium-source (>= 142.0)

debian/rules will change to:
    --chromium-archive=/usr/src/chromium/chromium.tar.xz

The manual chromium tarball setup documented in this file will no longer be
needed.

NOTE: Bundled libc++ will STILL be required even with chromium-source package,
due to fundamental ABI incompatibility with system libc++.

================================================================================
INTERIM BUILD DEPENDENCIES (Until Debian Packages Available)
================================================================================

This section documents workarounds required while waiting for proper Debian
packages for depot_tools and chromium-source. These are TEMPORARY solutions
that should be removed once the dependencies are available as Debian packages.

**IMPORTANT**: This package is designed to build WITHOUT network access once
all required files are in place. The debian/rules file handles all initialization
automatically - no manual setup should be required beyond placing the tarballs.

5. RUST-TOOLCHAIN TARBALL (Rust Standard Library Sources)
----------------------------------------------------------

Status: BUNDLED TARBALL (rust-toolchain.tar.xz)
Reason: Debian's rust-src package (1.90.0) lacks newer stdlib files required by Chromium
Future: When Debian rust-src matches Chromium's requirements, this can be removed

TECHNICAL DETAILS:

Chromium requires specific Rust standard library source files that are NEWER
than what Debian's rust-src package (1.90.0) provides. Missing files include:
  - sync/nonpoison/condvar.rs, rwlock.rs (newer std library features)
  - Other internal stdlib implementation files

The rust-toolchain.tar.xz contains the EXACT Rust stdlib version that Chromium
expects. This is extracted from Google Cloud Storage (GCS) during initial setup.

LOCATION AND SETUP:

The tarball MUST be placed in the PARENT directory of the source package:

    /path/to/workspace/
    ├── chromium-embedded-framework/     <- Source package directory
    │   ├── debian/
    │   ├── cef/
    │   └── ...
    ├── chromium_143.0.7499.40.orig.tar.xz  <- Chromium source
    └── rust-toolchain.tar.xz               <- THIS FILE (~142 MB)

HOW TO OBTAIN:

Option 1 - Download from GCS (requires network, one-time):

    # Find the URL in chromium/src/DEPS file, search for "rust-toolchain"
    # Example URL (version may change):
    $ wget "https://commondatastorage.googleapis.com/aspect-aspect-build-toolchain/aspect-aspect-build-toolchain-22.1.128/aspect_build_rust_toolchain_for_x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu.tar.xz" \
           -O rust-toolchain.tar.xz

Option 2 - Copy from existing build:

    If you have a previous successful build, the tarball may be cached:
    $ cp /path/to/previous/rust-toolchain.tar.xz ../

WHAT debian/rules DOES:

Step 7e in debian/rules extracts ONLY the library/ directory from this tarball:
  - Destination: third_party/rust-toolchain/lib/rustlib/src/rust/library/
  - Contains: alloc, core, std, and all stdlib crates at correct versions
  - Also creates stubs for cross-platform files (cygwin, darwin) that exist
    only to satisfy Rust's module resolution (never actually used on Linux)

MIGRATION PLAN:

When Debian's rust-src package is updated to include all files Chromium needs:
  1. Remove rust-toolchain.tar.xz requirement from this documentation
  2. Update debian/rules to use /usr/src/rustc-*/library/ instead
  3. Add rust-src to Build-Depends in debian/control

6. LLVM/CLANG VERSION LOCK (Currently LLVM-19)
-----------------------------------------------

Status: VERSION-LOCKED to LLVM-19
Reason: Debian sid currently provides clang-19 as the primary clang version
Impact: Several paths and patches are hardcoded to llvm-19

AFFECTED FILES:

1. debian/control - Build-Depends:
     clang-19, clang-format-19:native, lld-19, libclang-rt-19-dev,
     libc++-19-dev, libc++abi-19-dev

2. debian/rules - CLANG_MVERS variable (line ~33):
     CLANG_MVERS := 19

   This variable is used throughout debian/rules for:
     - clang_base_path="/usr/lib/llvm-$(CLANG_MVERS)"
     - PATH includes /usr/lib/llvm-$(CLANG_MVERS)/bin
     - LIBCLANG_PATH=/usr/lib/llvm-$(CLANG_MVERS)/lib

3. debian/patches/chromium/0012-bindgen-libclang-abspath.chromium.patch:
     Hardcodes: /usr/lib/llvm-19/lib

   This patch fixes bindgen's libclang discovery. The GN build system passes
   --libclang-path /usr/lib (from rust_bindgen_root="/usr"), but libclang.so
   is actually at /usr/lib/llvm-19/lib/libclang-19.so.19 on Debian.

   The patch intercepts /usr/lib and replaces it with /usr/lib/llvm-19/lib.

LIBCLANG DEPENDENCY:

The rust-bindgen tool requires libclang.so to parse C++ headers. On Debian:
  - Package: libclang1-19 (pulled in by clang-19)
  - Location: /usr/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/libclang-19.so.19
  - Symlink: /usr/lib/llvm-19/lib/libclang-19.so.19 -> actual file

WHY THE PATCH IS NEEDED:

Chromium's GN build system uses rust_bindgen_root to determine BOTH:
  1. bindgen executable path: ${rust_bindgen_root}/bin/bindgen
  2. libclang library path: ${rust_bindgen_root}/lib

On Debian:
  - bindgen is at /usr/bin/bindgen (so rust_bindgen_root="/usr" works)
  - libclang is at /usr/lib/llvm-19/lib (NOT /usr/lib)

The patch in run_bindgen.py detects when libclang_path=="/usr/lib" and
redirects to /usr/lib/llvm-19/lib where the library actually exists.

UPDATING TO A NEW LLVM VERSION:

When Debian moves to a new LLVM version (e.g., LLVM-20):

1. Update debian/control Build-Depends:
     sed -i 's/clang-19/clang-20/g; s/llvm-19/llvm-20/g' debian/control

2. Update debian/rules:
     Change: CLANG_MVERS := 19
     To:     CLANG_MVERS := 20

3. Update patch 0012-bindgen-libclang-abspath.chromium.patch:
     Change: /usr/lib/llvm-19/lib
     To:     /usr/lib/llvm-20/lib

   Or better: Make patch use a more dynamic approach if upstream accepts it.

4. Test build thoroughly - LLVM version changes can introduce new warnings
   or errors that require additional patches.

7. DEPOT_TOOLS INITIALIZATION (Automatic)
------------------------------------------

Status: HANDLED AUTOMATICALLY by debian/rules
Reason: depot_tools requires initialization before first use

WHAT debian/rules DOES:

Step 6.6.0.5 - Initialize depot_tools if needed:
  - Checks if python3_bin_reldir.txt exists (marker for initialized depot_tools)
  - If missing, runs: ./update_depot_tools (creates necessary wrapper scripts)
  - This is needed because bundled depot_tools may not be fully initialized

Step 6.6.0.6 - Create Python symlink:
  - depot_tools expects a bundled Python at specific location
  - We create a symlink to system Python: /usr/bin/python3
  - Location: depot_tools/bootstrap-2@*/python3/bin/python3

WHY THIS IS NEEDED:

depot_tools was designed to download its own Python from Google's servers.
In Debian builds (network-isolated), we use system Python instead. The
symlink makes depot_tools find Python at the path it expects.

NO MANUAL ACTION REQUIRED:

These steps run automatically during build. This documentation exists to
explain what happens if debugging is needed.

8. AUTONINJA PATH FIX (Patch 0016)
-----------------------------------

Status: PATCHED via 0016-use-full-path-autoninja-dpkg.cef.patch
Reason: dpkg-buildpackage environment may not have depot_tools in PATH

TECHNICAL DETAILS:

CEF's automate-git.py calls 'autoninja' by name, expecting it in PATH.
During dpkg-buildpackage, PATH setup can be incomplete, causing:

    subprocess.CalledProcessError: Command 'autoninja' not found

SOLUTION:

Patch 0016 modifies automate-git.py to use the full path to autoninja:
    autoninja_path = os.path.join(depot_tools_dir, 'autoninja')

This ensures autoninja is found regardless of PATH configuration.

================================================================================
BUILD ENVIRONMENT DIFFERENCES (Troubleshooting)
================================================================================

When moving this package to a new build machine, you may encounter issues
due to environment differences. This section documents known issues.

ISSUES DISCOVERED DURING MIGRATION:

1. depot_tools not initialized
   Symptom: "python3_bin_reldir.txt not found"
   Solution: debian/rules Step 6.6.0.5 now handles this automatically

2. Bundled Python not present
   Symptom: Python not found in depot_tools/bootstrap-*/
   Solution: debian/rules Step 6.6.0.6 creates symlink to system Python

3. autoninja not in PATH
   Symptom: "Command 'autoninja' returned non-zero exit status 127"
   Solution: Patch 0016 uses full path to autoninja

4. clang/clang++ not in PATH
   Symptom: "clang++: command not found"
   Solution: debian/rules adds /usr/lib/llvm-19/bin to PATH

5. libclang.so not found by bindgen
   Symptom: "Unable to find libclang: couldn't find any valid shared libraries"
   Solution: Patch 0012 redirects /usr/lib to /usr/lib/llvm-19/lib

6. rust-toolchain.tar.xz missing
   Symptom: "tar: rust-toolchain.tar.xz: Cannot open: No such file or directory"
   Solution: Download tarball and place in parent directory (see section 5)

CHECKLIST FOR NEW BUILD MACHINES:

Before building, verify:

[ ] Chromium tarball exists: ../chromium_143.0.7499.40.orig.tar.xz
[ ] Rust toolchain exists: ../rust-toolchain.tar.xz
[ ] Build dependencies installed: sudo apt build-dep chromium-embedded-framework
[ ] Sufficient disk space: ~40GB free
[ ] depot_tools directory exists (bundled in source package)

All other initialization is handled automatically by debian/rules.

BUILD TIME EXPECTATIONS:

Hardware significantly affects build time. The build is CPU-bound and highly
parallelizable - more cores = faster builds. Memory affects parallel job count.

| Hardware | Cores | RAM | Expected Time |
|----------|-------|-----|---------------|
| High-end desktop (2024, Ryzen 9/i9) | 16-24 | 64GB+ | 30-45 minutes |
| Mid-range desktop (2022) | 8-12 | 32GB | 1-2 hours |
| Laptop (2019, i7 quad-core) | 4-8 | 32GB | 12-24 hours |
| CI/CD standard runner | 2-4 | 8GB | 6-12 hours |
| CI/CD large runner | 8-16 | 32GB | 2-4 hours |
| Debian buildd (estimated) | 8-16 | 32GB | 3-6 hours |

NOTES:
- GPU does NOT accelerate compilation (CPU-bound workload)
- ninja uses (cores - 2) parallel jobs by default
- Low RAM causes swapping, dramatically increasing build time
- CI/CD runners are often shared/throttled, adding overhead
- Debian autobuilders vary significantly in hardware specs

