================================================================================
RUST VENDOR CRATE DEPENDENCIES (Build 175+)
================================================================================

4. RUST VENDOR CRATES (Hybrid Approach)
----------------------------------------

Status: HYBRID - 67% system packages + 33% bundled crates
Reason: MAXIMIZE Debian Policy compliance while handling unavailable crates

TECHNICAL DETAILS:

Chromium's Rust standard library build requires 130 vendor crates, normally
downloaded as part of rust-toolchain GCS tarball (142 MB, blocked in Debian).

ANALYSIS RESULTS (Build 175j - 2025-11-16):

Total vendor crates required: 130

Breakdown:
  ✅ Exact match in Debian:     11 crates (8%)
  ⚠️  Version mismatch:          76 crates (58%)  → Tested compatible via semver
  ❌ Not in Debian:             43 crates (33%)  → Requires bundling
  ────────────────────────────────────────────
  System packages available:    87 crates (67%)  ← SIGNIFICANT Debian compliance

IMPLEMENTED SOLUTION - HYBRID APPROACH:

Phase 1: System Packages (87 crates from Debian)

  Source: /usr/share/cargo/registry/
  Packages: librust-*-dev (see debian/control Build-Depends)

  Key packages:
    - librust-libc-dev (CRITICAL - tested in Build 175)
    - librust-object-dev (previous blocker, now resolved)
    - librust-serde-dev, librust-tokio-dev (common dependencies)
    - ... (84 more - see generate-build-depends.sh output)

  Implementation:
    debian/rules creates symlinks from system packages to:
      third_party/rust-toolchain/lib/rustlib/src/rust/library/vendor/

Phase 2: Bundled Crates (43 crates NOT in Debian)

  Location: debian/vendor-crates/
  Source: Extracted from rust-toolchain GCS tarball
  Size: ~5-8 MB (vs 142 MB for full tarball)

  Categories of bundled crates:

    ICU internationalization (~7 crates):
      - icu_calendar, icu_calendar_data, icu_collections
      - icu_locale, icu_locale_core, icu_locale_data, icu_provider

    Temporal/timezone (~4 crates):
      - temporal_rs, temporal_capi, timezone_provider
      - calendrical_calculations

    Font/text processing (~3 crates):
      - font-types, read-fonts, skrifa

    Diplomat FFI (~3 crates):
      - diplomat, diplomat-core, diplomat-runtime

    Architecture-specific (~5 crates):
      - arm, powerpc, loongarch64, wasm32, x86

    Audio codecs (~5 crates):
      - symphonia-*, codec_vendor, typed_codec_vendor

    Other utilities (~16 crates):
      - See RUST_VENDOR_ANALYSIS_*.md for complete list

  Implementation:
    debian/rules creates symlinks from debian/vendor-crates/ to:
      third_party/rust-toolchain/lib/rustlib/src/rust/library/vendor/

JUSTIFICATION FOR BUNDLING (Debian Policy §4.13 exception):

1. REQUIRED for building: Chromium Rust components depend on these crates
2. NOT AVAILABLE in Debian: No librust-*-dev packages as of 2025-11-16
3. DEPENDENCIES of Rust stdlib: Not direct Chromium dependencies
4. NORMALLY from GCS: Upstream downloads from rust-toolchain tarball (blocked)
5. MAXIMIZE system packages: We use 87/130 = 67% from Debian (significant compliance)
6. MINIMIZE bundling: Only 43/130 = 33% bundled (vs 100% if pure bundle approach)

COMPARISON WITH ALTERNATIVES:

| Approach | System Pkgs | Bundled | Policy Compliance | Size |
|----------|-------------|---------|-------------------|------|
| Pure System (Option C) | 87 (67%) | 0 (0%) | ✅ Best | 0 MB |
| HYBRID (Implemented) | 87 (67%) | 43 (33%) | ✅ Good | ~5-8 MB |
| Pure Bundle (Option A) | 0 (0%) | 130 (100%) | ⚠️ Acceptable | ~15-20 MB |

Pure system approach was attempted but 43 crates simply don't exist in Debian.
Hybrid achieves 67% policy compliance vs 0% with pure bundle.

MIGRATION PLAN:

As Debian packages become available for bundled crates:

1. Monitor Debian NEW queue for newly packaged Rust crates
2. Test compatibility with new Debian package versions
3. Remove crate from debian/vendor-crates/ bundle
4. Add librust-*-dev to debian/control Build-Depends
5. Update documentation and statistics

TARGET: Migrate from 67% → 100% system packages over time

VERIFICATION:

To verify Rust vendor coverage after build starts:

  $ cd chromium-embedded-framework/
  $ ./verify-rust-coverage.sh

Expected output:
  ✅ 130 crates found (100% coverage)
  📦 87 from system packages (/usr/share/cargo/registry/)
  📁 43 from bundled crates (debian/vendor-crates/)

LINTIAN OVERRIDE:

This may trigger: embedded-library (for bundled Rust crates)

Override justified because:
  - 43 crates NOT available in Debian (verified via cargo-debstatus)
  - 67% of crates use system packages (significant compliance)
  - Build-time only dependencies (not in binary package)
  - Migration plan documented for future Debian package availability

IMPLEMENTATION DETAILS:

debian/rules symlink creation (in override_dh_auto_configure):

  # Phase 1: System packages (87 crates)
  for pkg in /usr/share/cargo/registry/*; do
      ln -sf $$pkg third_party/rust-toolchain/.../vendor/$$(basename $$pkg)
  done

  # Phase 2: Bundled crates (43 crates)
  for crate in debian/vendor-crates/*; do
      ln -sf $$(readlink -f $$crate) third_party/rust-toolchain/.../vendor/$$(basename $$crate)
  done

TOOLS PROVIDED:

  - analyze-rust-vendor-deps.sh: Reanalyze crate availability (reusable)
  - install-all-rust-deps.sh: Install 87 system packages
  - create-vendor-bundle.sh: Extract 43 missing crates from rust-toolchain
  - verify-rust-coverage.sh: Verify 130 crates available during build
  - generate-build-depends.sh: Generate debian/control Build-Depends entries

PRECEDENT:

Debian's src:chromium package ALSO uses system rustc but includes some
bundled Rust dependencies for unavailable crates. This is a common pattern
for large Rust-based projects in Debian.

